  "slug": "untitled-topic-e003",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report tunable cavity magnonics, epsilon-near-zero confinement, integrated polarizers, and near-unity frequency conversion in compact platforms.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is engineering the response of confined electromagnetic and spin waves in compact platforms, with emphasis on tunable or dynamically modulated behavior rather than fixed response. The CrCl3 tripartite van der Waals cavity magnonic device is experimental, using hierarchical anti-crossing to obtain reduced magnon loss and a tunable coupled-mode Hamiltonian. The inverse-designed transparent conducting oxide cavities are theoretical, operating at the epsilon-near-zero point to give stronger light confinement and interface-controlled dynamics. The MXene-derived TiO2 integrated polarizers on silicon photonics report a figure of merit of 17.5 and power tolerance above 2 W. The hollow-core fiber frequency conversion study is theoretical and proposes near-unity efficiency above 99 percent through transient molecular modulation. The items differ in method and maturity and do not share one mechanism; what is new is the focus on device-level control of loss, confinement, and conversion efficiency.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
